{
  "action": "question",
  "question_count": 1,
  "is_atomic": true,
  "atomic_questions": [
    "What is the objective of this packing plan — are you trying to minimize the number of containers used, maximize the total tons of goods shipped, or something else (and if it's a trade-off, which one takes priority)?"
  ],
  "rationale": "The response asks for a single policy choice regarding the optimization objective. Although it lists options and includes a conditional clause about priority, it seeks one unified answer defining the goal, so it counts as one atomic question."
}

{
  "action": "question",
  "question_count": 1,
  "is_atomic": true,
  "atomic_questions": [
    "Do all available goods have to be shipped (i.e., every unit of A, B, C, D, E must be loaded into some container), or is it acceptable to leave some units unshipped as long as the containers that are used satisfy the rules?"
  ],
  "rationale": "The response asks a single clarification question regarding whether all goods must be shipped or if some can be left unshipped."
}

{
  "action": "question",
  "question_count": 1,
  "is_atomic": true,
  "atomic_questions": [
    "Are the goods units indivisible (each unit must be a whole number, so container loads are integer counts of units), or can units be split fractionally?"
  ],
  "rationale": "The response asks a single clarification question regarding the divisibility of goods units."
}

{
  "action": "question",
  "question_count": 1,
  "is_atomic": true,
  "atomic_questions": [
    "Is there a fixed number of containers available to choose from (i.e., a fleet size that caps how many containers you can use), or is the number of containers effectively unlimited and you simply want to use as few as possible?"
  ],
  "rationale": "The response asks for a single policy choice regarding the availability of containers (fixed cap vs. unlimited), which constitutes one semantically independent question."
}

{
  "action": "question",
  "question_count": 1,
  "is_atomic": true,
  "atomic_questions": [
    "The rule \"if a container contains any type A then it must also contain at least one type C\" — does this apply only to containers that are actually used (i.e., loaded with goods), or should it also be enforced in a way that affects how you decide which containers to open?"
  ],
  "rationale": "The response contains a single clarification question asking for a policy choice regarding the scope of a specific rule."
}

{
  "action": "question",
  "question_count": 1,
  "is_atomic": true,
  "atomic_questions": [
    "The \"at least 12 units of type D per container\" rule — does this apply to every used container, or only to containers that actually contain type D goods?"
  ],
  "rationale": "The response contains a single clarification question asking for a binary choice regarding the scope of a specific rule."
}

{
  "action": "question",
  "question_count": 1,
  "is_atomic": true,
  "atomic_questions": [
    "Is there any limit on how many units of a single goods type can go into one container (e.g., a container could hold all 120 units of A), or is the only per-container restriction the 18–60 ton weight range plus the D and A→C rules?"
  ],
  "rationale": "The response asks a single clarification question regarding per-container unit limits versus weight and rule-based restrictions."
}

{
  "action": "question",
  "question_count": 1,
  "is_atomic": true,
  "atomic_questions": [
    "The 18-ton minimum and 60-ton maximum per used container — are these strict (i.e., a container must weigh strictly more than 18 tons and strictly less than 60 tons), or are they inclusive (a container weighing exactly 18 tons or exactly 60 tons is acceptable)?"
  ],
  "rationale": "The response asks for a single policy choice regarding whether the weight bounds are strict or inclusive, which constitutes one semantically independent question."
}
